patch(b+'disc-graphs.md',[
('which problems are easy and which are hopeless.', 'which problems have efficient general algorithms and which have no known polynomial-time solution.'),
('A **path** is a sequence of vertices joined by edges; a **cycle** is a path that returns to its start without repeating edges or other vertices.', 'A walk is a sequence of vertices joined by edges. A simple path repeats no vertex; a cycle closes a path by returning to its start, with no other repeated vertices. An Euler trail may revisit vertices but never edges.'),
('Most real graphs are sparse (m much smaller than n²), so adjacency lists are the default.', 'For sparse graphs (m much smaller than n²), adjacency lists save space. The table assumes unsorted neighbor lists; hash-set or sorted representations change membership costs.'),
('For a graph with n vertices, these are all equivalent:', 'For a nonempty finite simple undirected graph with n vertices, these are equivalent:'),
('There is exactly one path between every pair of vertices.', 'There is exactly one simple path between every pair of distinct vertices.'),
('Adding any edge to a tree creates exactly one cycle;', 'Adding an edge between previously nonadjacent vertices of a tree creates exactly one cycle;'),
('The number of labelled trees on n vertices is nⁿ⁻²', 'For n ≥ 2, the number of labelled trees on a fixed n-vertex set is nⁿ⁻²'),
('For directed graphs, the condition is that every vertex has in-degree equal to out-degree (for a circuit).', 'For a directed Euler circuit with at least one edge, each vertex must have equal in-degree and out-degree, and all non-isolated vertices must belong to one connected component when directions are ignored. Balance alone is insufficient.'),
('and in generating de Bruijn sequences that test every possible keypad code with the fewest key presses.', 'and in generating de Bruijn sequences. Those give minimal universal strings for a sliding-window code tester; a keypad that requires Enter, resets after failure or locks out attempts needs a different model.'),
('as is the travelling salesman problem (the cheapest Hamiltonian cycle in a weighted graph).', 'as is the decision version of travelling salesman (is there a tour of cost at most B?). Finding a cheapest tour is an NP-hard optimization problem.'),
("Chaitin's allocator (1982) introduced this approach, and its descendants are still used in production compilers.", 'Chaitin and colleagues described an implemented graph-colouring allocator in their 1981 paper Register allocation via coloring.'),
('Since every face of a simple planar graph (with at least 3 vertices) is bounded by at least 3 edges and every edge borders at most 2 faces, 3F ≤ 2E.', 'For a connected simple plane graph with at least 3 vertices, each face boundary walk has length at least 3. Counting edge sides with multiplicity (a bridge is traversed twice on the same face) gives total boundary length 2E, hence 3F ≤ 2E.'),
('Euler circuits exist exactly when every degree is even (Euler paths: 0 or 2 odd vertices), and are found in linear time; Hamiltonian cycles are NP-complete.', 'When all edge-bearing vertices are connected, an undirected Euler circuit exists exactly when all degrees are even; an Euler trail needs 0 or 2 odd vertices. Construction is linear; deciding Hamiltonian-cycle existence is NP-complete.'),
('Planar graphs satisfy V − E + F = 2 and E ≤ 3V − 6, which proves K₅ and K₃,₃ non-planar.', 'A connected plane drawing satisfies V − E + F = 2. Simple planar graphs with V ≥ 3 satisfy E ≤ 3V − 6; the triangle-free bound E ≤ 2V − 4 is what rules out K₃,₃.')], 'Fix TSP decision/optimization classification, path terminology, Euler connectivity and planar proof assumptions, and register-allocation date.',src)
def graphs(q):
 q[0]['prompt']+=' Treat links as undirected point-to-point edges.'
 q[2]['workedExample']=q[2]['workedExample'].replace("The quadratic growth is why full meshes don't scale: 100 nodes need 4,950 links, and why systems use gossip protocols or hub topologies instead.", 'The edge count grows quadratically: 100 nodes require 4,950 pairwise links. Whether that is practical depends on physical versus logical connections and traffic; sparse overlays or hubs can reduce the connection count.')
 q[8]['workedExample']=q[8]['workedExample'].replace('In a simple planar graph with V ≥ 3, each face is bounded by at least 3 edges and each edge borders at most 2 faces, so 3F ≤ 2E.', 'For a connected simple plane graph with V ≥ 3, face boundary walks have length at least 3. Count edge sides with multiplicity, including bridges twice, to obtain 3F ≤ 2E.')
 q[9]['options'][0]['explanation']='Check that all edge-bearing vertices are connected and every degree is even, in O(n + m). Isolated vertices need not be visited by the edge-covering circuit.'
 q[10]['prompt']='In the simplified register-colouring model, four mutually interfering live values form K₄ and only 3 interchangeable registers are available. What must change?'
 q[10]['options'][0]['explanation']='K₄ needs four colours. Four vertices bound the chromatic number above by four, but do not make three sufficient.'
 q[10]['options'][2]['explanation']='With the fixed interference graph, three registers cannot hold all four values. Spill a value or transform the program/live ranges, for example by rematerializing a value when needed.'
 q[10]['workedExample']=q[10]['workedExample'].replace('The **register pressure** at a program point is the size of that clique.', 'In this simplified model the number of simultaneously live register-requiring values gives register pressure. Real targets add register classes, widths, fixed-register constraints and opportunities for rematerialization.')
 q[11]['workedExample']+='\nThe remaining edges can form a spanning tree only if the original graph connects these components; otherwise Kruskal ends with a spanning forest.'
questions(b+'disc-graphs.questions.json',graphs,'Clarify router and allocator models, planar edge-side counting, Euler isolated vertices and spanning-tree connectivity.',src)
