patch(b+'disc-number-theory.md',[
('Number theory used to be the textbook example of pure maths with no applications. Then public-key cryptography arrived, and now every HTTPS connection relies on it.', 'Number theory underpins public-key systems such as RSA and finite-field or elliptic-curve key exchange. HTTPS can use different TLS authentication and key-exchange modes; RSA is not required for every connection.'),
('C, C++, Java, JavaScript and Go truncate towards zero, giving `-7 % 3 == -1`. Code like `buckets[hash % n]` can then index out of range for a negative hash. In those languages use `((a % n) + n) % n`.', 'Modern C/C++, Java and Go integer division truncates towards zero. JavaScript Number division is floating-point, but its remainder also gives -7 % 3 == -1 (BigInt division truncates). A negative remainder may be an invalid index. For a positive modulus use Java Math.floorMod, or compute r = a % n and add n only when r < 0. Unconditionally adding n in ((a % n) + n) % n can overflow a fixed-width signed integer.'),
('unsigned 64-bit integers *are* arithmetic mod 2⁶⁴, which is why they wrap around.', 'unsigned 64-bit arithmetic in C/C++ wraps modulo 2⁶⁴. Other languages may use checked arithmetic or wider integers; signed C/C++ overflow is not defined as modular wraparound.'),
('gcd(a, b) is the largest integer dividing both.', 'For integers a and b not both zero, gcd(a, b) is their greatest positive common divisor. We use the programming convention gcd(0, 0) = 0.'),
('def gcd(a, b):\n    while b:', 'def gcd(a, b):\n    a, b = abs(a), abs(b)\n    while b:'),
('It is fast: every two steps the larger number at least halves, so it takes O(log min(a, b)) iterations.', 'For positive inputs, Euclid uses O(1 + log min(a, b)) remainder steps; one operand zero is an immediate case. This counts arithmetic operations, not their growing bit cost.'),
('The **extended Euclidean algorithm** finds them by running Euclid and tracking how each remainder is built from a and b:', 'The extended Euclidean algorithm finds them by tracking how each remainder is built from a and b. This recursive teaching version accepts non-negative integers; large worst-case inputs can exceed Python\'s recursion limit, so use an iterative implementation for those cases:'),
('That makes the integers mod p a **field**: you can add, subtract, multiply and divide.', 'That makes the residues mod p a field: addition, subtraction and multiplication are defined, and division by nonzero residues is possible.'),
('Elliptic-curve cryptography and Shamir\'s secret sharing typically compute in such prime fields. Reed–Solomon error correction (used in QR codes and RAID 6) uses a close cousin, the finite field GF(2⁸), whose elements fit in a byte.', 'Elliptic curves and Shamir secret sharing can use prime fields; binary extension fields are another option. Reed–Solomon codes work over finite fields, with GF(2⁸) a common byte-oriented choice. GF(2⁸) is not ordinary integer arithmetic modulo 256.'),
('```python\ndef primes_upto(n):\n    is_p', '```python\nfrom math import isqrt\n\ndef primes_upto(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return []\n    is_p'),
('int(n**0.5)', 'isqrt(n)'),
('Each round with a random base wrongly passes a composite with probability at most 1/4, so 40 rounds gives error below 2⁻⁸⁰. OpenSSL and most crypto libraries use it.', 'For a fixed odd composite, each independently uniform random base has a false-pass probability at most 1/4, so 40 independent rounds give at most 2⁻⁸⁰. This is a conditional false-pass bound, not the posterior probability that any accepted candidate is composite. OpenSSL documents trial division followed by Miller–Rabin rounds; its round policy is version-dependent.'),
('RSA is built on exactly that gap.', 'Factoring an RSA modulus breaks its private-key protection, but hardness of factoring alone is not a proof that every RSA-based scheme is secure.'),
('def modpow(a, e, n):\n    result = 1', 'def modpow(a, e, n):\n    if e < 0 or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError("e>=0, n>0 required")\n    result = 1 % n'),
('Python\'s built-in `pow(a, e, n)` does this (and more efficiently).', 'Python\'s built-in pow(a, e, n) performs efficient modular exponentiation and also supports invertible negative exponents. The teaching routine above is for integer e ≥ 0 and n > 0 and is not constant-time cryptographic code.'),
('Miller–Rabin adds an extra check that catches these.', 'Miller–Rabin adds stronger checks; base 2 rejects 561, but a single round is not guaranteed to reject every Carmichael number.'),
('Pick two large random primes p and q (each around 1024 bits or more).', 'For this mathematical two-prime construction, pick distinct odd primes p and q. Production key sizes and generation requirements must follow the chosen library and current deployment policy.'),
('**Encrypt** c = mᵉ mod n. **Decrypt** m = c^d mod n.', 'For an integer message representative 0 ≤ m < n, encrypt c = mᵉ mod n and decrypt m = c^d mod n.'),
('**Why it\'s secure (as far as we know)**: computing d needs φ(n), which needs p and q, which needs factoring n. The best known classical algorithm (the general number field sieve) is sub-exponential but still infeasible for 2048-bit n. A large quantum computer running Shor\'s algorithm would break it, which is why post-quantum schemes are being deployed.', '**Security connection:** knowing p and q lets an attacker compute a private exponent. However, inverting the RSA function has not been proved equivalent to factoring, and attacks can target weak encoding, key generation, side channels or protocols without factoring n. Secure RSA schemes require their own assumptions and implementation protections. Shor\'s algorithm would factor n on a sufficiently capable fault-tolerant quantum computer.\n\n> [!note] Evidence gap\n> No universal RSA latency or hardware attack-time estimate is supplied: this lesson has no reproducible benchmark or deployment threat model for one.'),
('which is several times faster.', 'which reduces operand sizes and can accelerate decryption; the speedup depends on arithmetic, hardware and implementation.'),
('fixed-width integers are arithmetic mod 2ᵏ.', 'unsigned C/C++ k-bit arithmetic wraps modulo 2ᵏ; other language/type rules differ.'),
('it is secure because factoring is hard, but only with proper padding.', 'factoring n breaks the key, but secure use also depends on the scheme assumptions, encoding, randomness and implementation.')], 'Fix negative-modulo overflow advice, sieve edge case and modpow infinite loop, scope Miller–Rabin probability, and remove false RSA factoring equivalence/unsourced timing.',srcn)
def number(q):
 q[0]['options'][0]['explanation']='Modern C and Java integer division gives quotient -2 and remainder -1. JavaScript Number division instead gives a noninteger quotient; Python // floors to -3.'
 q[0]['workedExample']=q[0]['workedExample'].replace('`h % n` is always a valid index in Python.', '`h % n` is a valid index into an n-element sequence when h is an integer and n > 0.')
 q[3]['options'][1]['text']='The ordinary rational number 1/7, without choosing a residue'
 q[3]['options'][1]['explanation']='Here an inverse is a residue class, conventionally represented by an integer 0 through 39. Modular notation 1/7 can denote that inverse when defined, but the ordinary rational 1/7 is not the requested representative.'
 q[6]['workedExample']=q[6]['workedExample'].replace('Each multiplication is on 2048-bit numbers, which is why RSA private-key operations take on the order of a millisecond, and why implementations use CRT to work with half-size numbers.', 'Operand size is determined by the modulus; if that is also 2048 bits, residues have at most 2048 bits and products can be wider before reduction. CRT uses smaller moduli. No latency follows without measuring a specified implementation and machine.')
 q[9]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. 561 = 3·11·17 is composite and passes Fermat for every coprime base. Miller–Rabin base 2 rejects 561; other bases can pass, so use a suitable tested primality routine.'
 q[9]['workedExample']=q[9]['workedExample'].replace('Each round errs with probability at most 1/4, even on Carmichael numbers.', 'For each fixed odd composite, an independently uniform random base has false-pass probability at most 1/4, including Carmichael numbers.')
 q[10]['options'][1]['explanation']='The raw RSA primitive is multiplicative modulo n. OAEP encoding and validation prevent treating arbitrary products of ciphertexts as valid encryptions of corresponding application-message products; they do not change the primitive\'s algebra.'
 q[10]['options'][4]['explanation']='65537 is a conventional public exponent and is not inherently insecure. Security also needs valid key generation, a suitable scheme and correct implementation; padding is not the only possible failure point.'
 q[11]['prompt']+=' Assume a nonempty shard array.'
 q[11]['options'][1]['explanation']='For positive n, Math.floorMod(h, n) gives 0 ≤ r < n. The handwritten expression ((h % n) + n) % n can overflow when n is large, so it is not a safe general replacement.'
 q[11]['workedExample']=q[11]['workedExample'].replace('Python never has this problem since its `%` already floors.', 'For a positive modulus, Python integer % already gives a non-negative remainder. In Java, unconditional remainder-plus-n normalization can itself overflow; Math.floorMod avoids that trap.')
